 ],
 "literal": "If bathing in water for a long time, even the eyes will turn red. / In the mouth of those who are satiated, even honey will taste sour. / If you have separated from us, please deliver us back to our house, / in our town of my father with beautiful cool ponds, / on the street where fierce snakes roam, / us, whose shivering grief and distress you once removed.",
 "translation": "If one bathes too long in water, even the eyes will turn red. To those who have eaten their fill, even sweet honey will taste sour. If you must leave us now, please return us to our home in our father's town, which has beautiful cool ponds—back to the street where fierce snakes roam, where you once saved us from shivering grief and distress.",
 "modern": "நீரில் நீண்ட நேரம் விளையாடினால் கண்களும் சிவந்துவிடும். வயிறார உண்டவர்களின் வாய்க்குத் தேனும் புளிக்கும். எங்களைப் பிரிந்து செல்ல நினைத்தால், குளிர்ந்த பொய்கைகளையுடைய என் தந்தையின் ஊரில், கொடிய பாம்புகள் நடமாடும் தெருவில், அன்று எங்கள் நடுக்கத்தையும் துன்பத்தையும் போக்கிய எங்களை, எங்கள் இல்லத்திற்கே கொண்டுபோய் விட்டுவிடுங்கள்.",
 "explanation": "In this Marutam poem, the heroine's friend addresses the hero who has grown indifferent and is preparing to leave. She begins with two universal truths: even refreshing water makes the eyes red if one bathes too long, and even sweet honey tastes sour to someone who is already full. These analogies subtly criticize the hero, suggesting that he has grown tired of the heroine's love through over-familiarity. She then asks him, if he must depart, to return the heroine safely to her father's house. By reminding him of the street where fierce snakes roam—the very place where he used to meet her in secret to dispel her shivering fears—the friend evokes his past devotion to make him feel guilty about his current neglect. The mention of cool ponds establishes the fertile Marutam landscape, contrasting its outer beauty with the heroine's inner distress.",
